canonical_claim.statement:
A model that satisfies everything Section 3 and Condition 4.1 require and in which the EPO does not exist. Let $N=1$, $\Omega=\mathbb R$ with the power set as event space, $\mathbf Z(\omega)=\omega$ (a bijection onto $\mathbb R^1$, so $\mathcal Z=\mathbb R$), and $$p(z)=\begin{cases}1/2&z=0,\\2^{-k-1}&z=k\text{ for an integer }k\ge1,\\0&\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}$$ Let $g_1(z)=1$ if $z$ is a positive integer and $g_1(z)=0$ otherwise, and $$y_1(z)=\begin{cases}(-1)^{k+1}2^{k+1}&z=k\text{ for an integer }k\ge1,\\0&\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}$$ Then:
(a) $p\ge0$, $\sum_zp(z)=1$, $\Pr[A]=\sum_{z\in A}p(z)$ is a probability measure on the power set, $\mathrm{Supp}(\mathbf Z)=\{0,1,2,\ldots\}$, and every raw potential outcome is a finite real number;
(b) $\pi_1(1)=\pi_1(0)=\tfrac12$, so Condition 4.1 holds for $d=1$ and $d'=0$ with $c=\tfrac12$;
(c) $y_1(k)\,p(k)=(-1)^{k+1}$ for every integer $k\ge1$; hence, for every $m\ge1$, $\sum_{k=1}^{2m}y_1(k)^+p(k)=m$, $\sum_{k=1}^{2m}y_1(k)^-p(k)=m$ and $\sum_{k=1}^{2m}|y_1(k)|\,p(k)=2m$, and the partial sums of $\sum_ky_1(k)p(k)$ in increasing order of $k$ are 1 after an odd number of terms and 0 after an even number;
(d) therefore the numerator $\sum_{z\in\mathrm{Supp}(\mathbf Z):g_1(z)=1}y_1(z)\Pr[\mathbf Z=z]$ of Equation (5) is not summable (its positive and negative parts are both $+\infty$, and the series ordered by $k$ does not converge), so $\overline y_1(1)=\mathrm E[y_1(\mathbf Z)\mid D_1=1]$ does not exist; with $N=1$ the AEPO $\overline y(1)$ of Equation (6), the EED $\tau_1(1,0)$ of Equation (7) and the AEED $\tau(1,0)$ of Equation (8) do not exist either, while $\overline y_1(0)=0$;
(e) the Horvitz–Thompson estimator of supplement Equation (1) is $\hat{\overline y}(1)=2\,Y_1\mathbb 1\{D_1=1\}$, a finite real number at every assignment, but $\mathrm E[\hat{\overline y}(1)^+]=\mathrm E[\hat{\overline y}(1)^-]=+\infty$, so $\mathrm E[\hat{\overline y}(1)]$ does not exist and supplement Equation (3) does not hold as an identity between finite expectations.

Corrected statement (Sol's smallest repair). In the setting of Section 3 (a finite population of $N$ units; a design with countable support $\mathrm{Supp}(\mathbf Z)$; real raw potential outcomes $y_i$ on $\mathcal Z$; exposure mappings $g_i$), let $d$ be an exposure with $\pi_i(d)>0$ for every $i\in\{1,\ldots,N\}$ (Condition 4.1) and suppose in addition that, for every unit $i$, $$M_{i,d}:=\sum_{\mathbf z\in\mathrm{Supp}(\mathbf Z):g_i(\mathbf z)=d}|y_i(\mathbf z)|\Pr[\mathbf Z=\mathbf z]<\infty.$$ Then:
(i) the numerator of Equation (5) converges absolutely, $\overline y_i(d)$ is a finite real number equal to $\mathrm E[y_i(\mathbf Z)\mid D_i=d]$, and $\mathrm E[|Y_i|\mid D_i=d]=M_{i,d}/\pi_i(d)$;
(ii) the AEPO $\overline y(d)$ of Equation (6) is a finite real number; if the same two conditions hold for a second exposure $d'$, the EED $\tau_i(d,d')$ of Equation (7) and the AEED $\tau(d,d')$ of Equation (8) are finite real numbers and $\tau(d,d')=\overline y(d)-\overline y(d')=\frac1N\sum_{i=1}^N\tau_i(d,d')$;
(iii) $T_i:=Y_i\mathbb 1\{D_i=d\}/\pi_i(d)$ is integrable with $\mathrm E|T_i|=M_{i,d}/\pi_i(d)$ and $\mathrm E[T_i]=\overline y_i(d)$, so the Horvitz–Thompson estimator of supplement Equation (1) satisfies $\mathrm E[\hat{\overline y}(d)]=\overline y(d)$; with the conditions for both $d$ and $d'$, $\mathrm E[\hat{\overline y}(d)-\hat{\overline y}(d')]=\tau(d,d')$. No SUTVA or NURVA assumption is used;
(iv) the condition $M_{i,d}<\infty$ is automatic when $\mathrm{Supp}(\mathbf Z)$ is finite, and when NURVA (Condition 5.1) holds at the exposure $d$: then $y_i(\mathbf z)$ takes a single value $a_i$ on $\{\mathbf z\in\mathrm{Supp}(\mathbf Z):g_i(\mathbf z)=d\}$ and $M_{i,d}=|a_i|\,\pi_i(d)$.
Equations (5)–(8) are unchanged; only the general existence claims and the unrestricted first-moment unbiasedness claim are qualified.
